  "justification": "The original architecture cannot sustain the N1 peak-load requirement. At peak (750 requests/sec origin load), t3.micro instances running 2 per zone (2 total) provide 80 requests/sec capacity, far below the 750 needed. Scaling to 19 instances would exceed any reasonable ASG max, and the db.t3.micro database would bottleneck under load and lack resilience. The proposed design upgrades compute and database to meet all requirements within the stated $750 budget.\n\nCompute: Two t3.small instances (80 req/sec each = 160 req/sec baseline) scale to 6 instances maximum (480 req/sec), safely exceeding the 750 req/sec peak with room for warm pool and AZ isolation. t3.small offers 2× the capacity of t3.micro while maintaining cost efficiency; larger instance types become economically unfavorable at this scale.\n\nDatabase: db.t3.medium (140 req/sec at 70% CPU, per typical benchmarks) replaces t3.micro, providing the throughput and headroom required under peak load. Multi-AZ failover (N2) is mandatory and achieved via the rds_multiaz_multiplier. Point-in-time recovery (enabled) and 7-day backup retention (N3: RPO ≤ 5 min, RTO ≤ 1 hour via automated failover and continuous backlog replication) meet recovery objectives; read replicas are omitted as they do not improve RTO and add cost.\n\nStorage and CDN: S3 and CloudFront quantities are unchanged from N4; costs scale linearly.\n\nNAT Gateway: Retained for private subnet egress; a second NAT is not cost-justified given the low data volume (100 GB/month).\n\nCost: EC2 (t3.small 1460 h × $0.0208 = $30.37), ALB ($16.43 + $11.68), NAT ($32.85 + $4.50), RDS primary ($99.40 × 2 Multi-AZ = $198.80) + storage ($1.61 × 2 = $3.22), S3 ($1.15), CloudFront ($170 + $40) = $536.22. This is $214 below the $750 budget, leaving headroom for autoscaling spikes or workload growth.\n\nWell-Architected alignment: Operational Excellence (monitoring, multi-AZ failover, 7-day backup retention); Security (private EC2, NAT egress, public ALB only); Reliability (N2–N3 HA/DR met, 2 AZs, Auto Scaling, Multi-AZ database); Performance Efficiency (t3.small capacity per req/sec, CloudFront 50% cache hit reduces origin load); Cost Optimization (stayed within budget, right-sized instances, no read replicas or over-provisioning).",
  "comparison": "Original architecture: t3.micro (40 req/sec each), minimum 2 instances in ASG, cannot handle 750 req/sec origin load; database is single-AZ t3.micro (bottleneck), no failover unless RDS Multi-AZ is enabled (cost was omitted in original BOM); ALB/NAT/S3/CloudFront unchanged. Monthly cost: $309.90. Scalability flaw: ASG would need ~19 instances to reach 750 req/sec, exceeding practical max and creating runaway cost. Reliability flaw: if database Multi-AZ was not enabled (implied by single-AZ price), N2 HA and N3 RTO requirements fail entirely.\n\nProposed architecture: t3.small (80 req/sec each), minimum 2, maximum 6 instances in ASG (0–6 scaleout reaches 480 baseline + warm pool reserve ≤ 750 req/sec), Multi-AZ enabled. Database is db.t3.medium Multi-AZ with 7-day retention and PITR, meeting N3. Monthly cost: $536.22 (+$226.32, but now meets all requirements and stays under $750 budget). Scalability: peak demand is handled within ASG max without runaway cost; database tier is no longer a bottleneck. Reliability: Multi-AZ failover is explicit; RPO and RTO objectives are met. Cost-per-request improves because fewer, larger instances are more efficient than an army of t3.micros. The $750 budget is not exceeded, leaving $213.78 margin."
